# Question: what does the infrastructure for one customer cost a month (the
# shared platform divided among customers, plus what each one adds), and how
# likely is it to take more than 30% of the price they pay?
#
# Run it:
#   ubc --scenario cloud/cost-per-customer
# Change an assumption, for example twice as many customers on the same platform:
#   ubc --set 'customers = 1k to 3k' --scenario cloud/cost-per-customer
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens cost_per_customer --scenario cloud/cost-per-customer
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Amounts are in dollars a
# month. SaaS companies usually aim for a gross margin of 75-80%, and
# infrastructure is most of the cost of serving a customer.
#
# Sources: typical experience in SaaS companies; there is no published
# benchmark for these ranges, so replace them with your own bill and customer
# count.

# Assumptions
customers = 500 to 1.5k           # paying customers; from your billing system
platform = 20k to 50k             # the shared bill (clusters, databases, networking, tooling); look at your cloud bill
per_customer = 15 to 60           # what each customer adds (storage, emails, API calls); typical experience
price = 300                       # what a customer pays a month; your own price list

# Calculation
cost_per_customer = platform / customers + per_customer        # a share of the platform plus what the customer adds
infra_share = cost_per_customer / price                        # the share of the price that goes to infrastructure

# Result
cost_per_customer
infra_share
P(infra_share > 30%)              # how likely is infrastructure to take more than 30% of the price?

# Output (seed 1, 5000 samples):
#   cost_per_customer = ≈ 69.7   90% [41.7 … 114]   98% [33 … 150]   ▃▄▆█▇▇▇▅▄▄▃▂▂▁▁▁
#   infra_share = ≈ 0.232   90% [0.139 … 0.382]   98% [0.11 … 0.49]   ▃▄▆█▇▇▇▅▄▄▃▂▂▁▁▁
#   P(infra_share > 30%) = 0.21
